      'What to build: a single placement rule, stated in radii, that every large body obeys — Sol, the heart worlds, other systems\' stars and giants, and black holes. Something of the shape "the gap between two large bodies is at least K x (R1 + R2)" plus "nothing foreign sits inside a system\'s outermost orbit plus a margin". Choose K from how it LOOKS (a neighbour giant should read as a distant world, a few degrees across, not a wall), state it, and make it one named constant Ben can tune. ORIGINAL\'s 28,000-41,000 nebula distances are a sound reference for where the nebula systems belong.',
      '**Black holes scale with the giants.** A black hole must dwarf the planets around it. The `blackholes` package (merged before you started) has already enlarged and restyled them; your job is their SPACING under the same rule, and the asteroid belts and systems that ring them.',
      'Everything that assumed the old distances must follow: the demo autopilot\'s routes and warp legs (KEEP: warp-arrival still parks with the planet big and dead ahead), nebula discovery paths, navigation list and distances, map/radar scale, culling ranges, enemy and trader spawn positions, music zones keyed to nebula distance. Warp exists for the long legs; a journey being longer is fine, a journey being impossible is not.',
      '',
      'Ben, verbatim: "I want the Sol System planets and Sun to be as big as the other ones in the game." And: "I also don\'t think the star-explorer build ever made the Sol System planets as big as the rest in the game, with all that amazing detail but I wanted it to."',
      '',
      '### Part 1 — Sol at full scale and detail (`solScale: big`)',
      '1. **Measure first.** Tabulate radius of the Sun, every Sol planet and major moon, and of stars and planets in several other systems (including procedural ones); and which shading features each group gets (atmosphere shell, limb/terminator shading, rings and ring shadows, surface detail, corona). Both tables go in your report.',
      '2. **Scale Sol up** into the same size class as its counterparts elsewhere, with sensible proportions between Sol bodies. Give Sol bodies the same detail and lighting the best bodies elsewhere get. Earth, Jupiter, Saturn and the Sun are showpieces.',
      '3. **Scale everything that depends on size**: orbit radii and spacing, moon orbits, rings, atmosphere shells, the asteroid belt, gravity wells and slingshot zones and halos, collision radii, the demo autopilot\'s warp-arrival parking distance (KEEP: still stops with the planet big and dead ahead), navigation distances, orbit lines, radar/map, culling and impostor thresholds, camera near/far, enemy spawn positions around Sol bodies. Grep for every hard-coded Sol distance. `solScale: overhaul` must give back today\'s sizes.',
      '',
      '### Part 2 — Outer systems and every other star',
      'Ben, verbatim: "Outer system planets and stars should be increased in scale as well." The Alpha / Beta / Gamma system stars near Sol are radius 10-14 and their planets smaller still (js/outer-systems.js, unchanged from ORIGINAL); stars in the nebula clusters are radius 40-70. Bring every star and planet in the game into ONE coherent size ladder — moons < planets < giants < stars < black holes — so nothing is a speck beside a heart world, and space them by the Part 0 rule. State the ladder as a small table of named constants.',
      '',
      'The opening in-game view is a SEPARATE package (`vista`) that runs after yours: leave the player\'s start position and orientation alone, but make sure the start point is not inside or dangerously near a scaled body.',
